WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.060
A campus café forecasts how many iced
lattes it will sell each week, and every

00:00:05.060 --> 00:00:07.560
single week, the forecast comes in too
low.

00:00:08.180 --> 00:00:11.040
Is that just bad luck, or is something
wrong with the forecast?

00:00:11.900 --> 00:00:12.860
Pause and decide.

00:00:13.960 --> 00:00:16.160
The café uses exponential smoothing.

00:00:16.820 --> 00:00:20.960
The new forecast equals the old forecast,
plus alpha times the last error.

00:00:21.660 --> 00:00:23.160
Alpha is the smoothing constant.

00:00:23.960 --> 00:00:28.180
Here it is 0.5, so each new forecast
closes half of the gap.

00:00:28.740 --> 00:00:32.660
In week one, the forecast was 40, and the
café sold 48.

00:00:33.460 --> 00:00:34.120
The error is 8.

00:00:34.740 --> 00:00:37.060
Half of 8 is 4, so the next forecast is
44.

00:00:37.960 --> 00:00:39.120
But sales climbed to 52.

00:00:39.980 --> 00:00:40.780
The error is 8 again.

00:00:41.520 --> 00:00:44.520
Each week the forecast steps up by 4, and
so do sales.

00:00:45.380 --> 00:00:46.760
The forecast never catches up.

00:00:47.560 --> 00:00:48.600
Now let's measure it.

00:00:49.420 --> 00:00:53.400
The mean absolute deviation, or MAD, is
the average size of the errors.

00:00:54.080 --> 00:00:57.820
Five errors of 8 give a MAD of 8, but MAD
ignores direction.

00:00:58.740 --> 00:01:03.220
A forecast that is 8 too low, then 8 too
high, has exactly the same MAD.

00:01:04.020 --> 00:01:06.880
To see direction, add up the errors and
keep their signs.

00:01:07.740 --> 00:01:09.680
Five errors of plus 8 add up to 40.

00:01:10.440 --> 00:01:12.580
The tracking signal divides that sum by
MAD.

00:01:13.300 --> 00:01:14.820
40 divided by 8 is 5.

00:01:15.620 --> 00:01:19.120
The control limits are plus and minus 4,
so 5 is out of bounds.

00:01:19.940 --> 00:01:21.080
This forecast is biased.

00:01:21.820 --> 00:01:23.920
Simple smoothing lags behind a trend.

00:01:24.820 --> 00:01:27.740
For the forecast that swings both ways,
the errors cancel.

00:01:28.660 --> 00:01:31.320
The sum is 8, and the tracking signal is
only 1.

00:01:32.240 --> 00:01:34.300
So MAD tells you how big the errors are.

00:01:34.860 --> 00:01:37.240
The tracking signal tells you whether they
lean one way.

00:01:37.860 --> 00:01:39.640
When it leaves the limits, fix the model.

00:01:40.780 --> 00:01:42.000
Raise alpha or add a trend.
